  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nA shortwave and associated weak surface low will track across \nthe Dakotas into MN and northern IA after midnight tonight. \nStrong moisture transport aided by a 45kt LLJ will allow for \nscattered showers and thunderstorms to track eastward early \nSaturday. Forecast soundings and RAP layer RH progs show this \nactivity will encounter quite a bit dry air once it arrives in \nour northwest. In addition, the terminus and orientation of the \nLLJ appears to support the bulk of these showers/isolated storms\nremaining to our north, but perhaps clipping the Hwy 20  \ncorridor near IIB before decaying further as LLJ wanes. Have \nmaintained chance/likely PoPs north of a Cedar Rapids to Dubuque \nline 12-18z Sat, but this may be too high. Any rain that reaches\nthe ground will be light and less than a tenth of an inch. Some\nCAMs develop some additional showers during the afternoon/evening\nacross eastern IA, but forecast soundings are rather dry below \n700mb and have kept the forecast dry for now. \n\nSomething that will be more noticeable through Saturday will be\nthe increasing humidity levels and breezy south winds. Forecast\nsoundings show 30kts at the top of the mixed layer resulting in\nsome gustiness during the afternoon hours. Despite WAA through\nthe day, cloud cover should keep northeast IA/northwest IL\ncooler in the upper 70s/low 80s, with mid to upper 80s\nelsewhere.\n\nSunday-Thursday...active pattern with several shortwaves topping\nthe strong 594 dam ridge leading to building heat and periodic \nchances for storms. Confidence in the timing of storms remains\nquite low due to the varying model solutions strength and \nbreakdown timing of the ridge. 00z NAEFS shows this ridge is in \nthe 99th percentile with respect to climatology, which is one of\nthe reasons why CPC has a moderate risk of excessive heat \nacross much of the south central CONUS. LREF probabilities for \n850 mb temps greater than 22C are now in the 60-80% range Monday\nthrough Thursday and NBM probabilities of 70+ degree dewpoints \nremain in the 60-80%. This will yield highs in the upper \n80s/lower 90s and heat indices in the mid 90s to near 100 \ndegrees. The WPC experimental heat risk product is in Major to \nExtreme categories Monday through Thursday along and south of \nI-80 and forecast wet bulb globe temps rise around 86 degrees in\nthe afternoon. Heat headlines may eventually be needed next \nweek for a portion of the area. In addition, some record warm \nlows may be in jeopardy and have included those in a climate \nsection below. \n\nRegarding storm potential, there is a Day 3 Marginal (level 1 \nout of 5) risk for severe storms Sunday north of a Ames to \nDubuque line. A warm front will be near the IA/MN border Sunday \nafternoon with building instability (MLCAPEs in the 1200-1700 \nJ/Kg) and more favorable kinematics (0-6 km shear values 40-50 \nkts) in the warm sector. An EML and marginal mid level lapse \nrates may limit storm development. If any were to develop, heavy\nrain and damaging wind would be the primary threats. As is \noften the case with this ring of fire pattern, assessing severe \nand heavy rain risk further out past Day 3 is too difficult at \nthis time range. Although some ML guidance is starting to show \nsome signs of low probabilities Tuesday and Wednesday. Something\nto watch in later forecasts.",
